# Copy to <repo>/CODEOWNERS (or .gitlab/CODEOWNERS) and replace the placeholders
# with real GitLab usernames or groups.
#
# READ THIS FIRST — GITLAB'S CODEOWNERS IS NOT GITHUB'S.
#
# 1. **It is a paid feature.** Code Owners requires GitLab Premium or Ultimate.
#    On Free the file is parsed and displayed and NOTHING IS ENFORCED. If this
#    project is on Free, this file is decoration: use the fallback at the bottom
#    of this comment instead, and say in CLAUDE.md which one you chose. A
#    protection you believe you have and do not is worse than none.
#
# 2. **Approval is not required until you require it.** Even on Premium, add the
#    rule explicitly: Settings → Merge requests → "All threads must be resolved"
#    is not it — you want Settings → Repository → Protected branches → main, and
#    then Settings → Merge requests → Merge request approvals → "Require approval
#    from Code Owners". Without that box the file is advisory.
#
# 3. **Sections change the semantics.** GitLab groups entries under `[Section]`
#    headings and requires approval per section. The single unnamed section below
#    behaves closest to GitHub's model. Do not add sections without re-reading
#    how optional (`^[Section]`) sections downgrade a rule to advisory.
#
# 4. **Last matching pattern wins, as on GitHub** — but GitLab matches the
#    patterns in a section independently, so a broad rule later in the file can
#    quietly replace a narrow one above it. Keep the narrow rules last.
#
# WHY ANY OF THIS: every artifact the gate's verdict rests on lives inside the
# repository, which means inside the perimeter the party being checked controls.
# The gate script defines what "passing" means. The pipeline decides whether the
# gate runs at all. The pin file is what makes a change to the gate visible. The
# hook configuration decides whether the package guard exists. All of them can be
# edited in the same merge request as the work they would excuse. Code ownership
# does not prevent those edits — it makes them require a named human's approval,
# the only trust anchor available that sits outside the perimeter.
#
# {{OWNER}} — e.g. @your-username, or @your-group/your-subgroup
# {{REVIEWER}} — someone who is not the usual author of the code, where one exists.

# --- the gate's own definition ---------------------------------------------
/gate.sh                    {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/gate.ps1                   {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.gate-sha256               {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/check-stubs.sh             {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/check-stubs.ps1            {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.gate-stubs-baseline       {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- whether the gate runs at all ------------------------------------------
# GitLab runs .gitlab-ci.yml from the source branch for a standard merge-request
# pipeline, so without this a merge request can weaken its own required check.
# tooling/ci/ is owned too: the pipeline file is a wrapper and every check it
# performs lives in tooling/ci/gate-ci.sh. Owning the wrapper and not the script
# it calls would leave the entire gate editable in silence.
/.gitlab-ci.yml             {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/CODEOWNERS                 {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/tooling/ci/                {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- whether the guards exist ----------------------------------------------
/.claude/settings.json      {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.claude/settings.local.json {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.claude/hooks/             {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- what the gate is allowed to let through --------------------------------
/docs/exceptions.md         {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- the rules themselves ---------------------------------------------------
/docs/process/              {{OWNER}}
/docs/stacks/               {{OWNER}}

# --- FALLBACK FOR GITLAB FREE ------------------------------------------------
# Code Owners is unavailable, so there is no path-scoped approval. The closest
# honest substitute, in descending order of strength:
#
#   1. Protect `main` with "Allowed to merge: Maintainers" and keep the people who
#      write the code as Developers. Merge then requires a second human, though
#      not a path-aware one.
#   2. Require 1 approval (Settings → Merge requests → Approvals) and add a rule
#      that approvals are reset when new commits are pushed. Without the reset, an
#      approval given before the gate was edited still counts after.
#   3. Say plainly in CLAUDE.md that the enforcement perimeter is unowned on this
#      project, so nobody later assumes it is covered. An undocumented gap gets
#      trusted past; a documented one gets budgeted for.
